./iterations/iter_005/promising_ideas.json:1:{"bibliography_candidates":[{"authors":"Alexander Ryabchenko, Idan Attias, Daniel M. Roy","relevance":"Useful for adaptive truncation/skipping directions because it characterizes delayed online learning with limited tracking capacity and distinguishes total delay, maximum delay, and outstanding-feedback constraints.","title":"Capacity-Constrained Online Learning with Delays: Scheduling Frameworks and Regret Trade-offs","url":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v291/ryabchenko25a.html","venue_year":"COLT / PMLR, 2025"},{"authors":"Saeed Masoudian, Julian Zimmert, Yevgeny Seldin","relevance":"Gives delayed-bandit skipping bounds and lower bounds for arbitrary delays; conceptually relevant to replacing raw tail cutoffs by skipped-feedback or outstanding-observation quantities.","title":"A Best-of-Both-Worlds Algorithm for Bandits with Delayed Feedback","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2206.14906","venue_year":"NeurIPS, 2022"},{"authors":"Haipeng Luo, Spandan Senapati, Vatsal Sharan","relevance":"Not directly about delays, but the simultaneous-regret viewpoint may be a useful analogy for formalizing simultaneous-prefix or multiple-horizon compatibility.","title":"Simultaneous Swap Regret Minimization via KL-Calibration","url":"https://openreview.net/forum?id=Ib4ZXPXpss","venue_year":"NeurIPS, 2025"},{"authors":"Shan Xue, Ye Du, Liang Xu","relevance":"Recent adjacent online-learning market-making paper with inventory constraints; useful background, though not a delayed-fill tail characterization result.","title":"Adaptive Market Making with Inventory Constraints via Online Learning","url":"https://doi.org/10.1609/aaai.v39i20.35492","venue_year":"AAAI, 2025"}],"ideas":[{"id":"idea_01","risks":"May only polish an already-known block obstruction; it will not by itself produce an instance-wise tail characterization. Strict-threshold suprema and old-reward caps must be checked carefully.","suggested_lenses":["proof audit","constant optimization","lower-bound formalization"],"summary":"Verify and generalize the solver's two-threshold block lemma for arbitrary 0 < c0 < c1 <= 1. Check the aggregate inequality, derive the exact lower-bound coefficient c0(c1-c0)/c1 per zero-round, optimize it at c0 = 1/2, c1 = 1, and then lift the lemma cleanly to both accepted concatenations: process-unaware universal blocks and same-path horizon-oblivious blocks.","title":"Audit The General Two-Threshold Lemma","why_promising":"This could unify the finite two-continuation and same-path two-horizon arguments into one reusable theorem block with sharp constants."},{"id":"idea_02","risks":"The modulus can become tautological if it is defined directly as regret. It may also be hard to extend beyond zero blocks and deterministic threshold continuations.","suggested_lenses":["definition design","linear programming duality","counterexample testing"],"summary":"Define a prefix-compatibility modulus on a common unrevealed prefix: given possible continuation thresholds c_j and horizons T_j, ask whether one empirical distribution of prefix quotes can be near-optimal for all continuations/prefixes simultaneously. For zero-block gadgets this becomes a small LP over quote distributions; test whether its dual recovers the two-threshold lower bound and whether it is small on sparse vanishing spikes and coded-delay examples.","title":"Prefix-Compatibility Modulus","why_promising":"It targets the real obstruction in the accepted lower bounds: incompatible prefix optima, not large delay tails alone."},{"id":"idea_03","risks":"Likely only gives sufficient conditions for process-aware or law-aware learners. Necessity may collapse into the definition of horizon stability.","suggested_lenses":["sufficiency theorem","algorithm design","stress tests"],"summary":"Develop an anytime sufficiency condition based on stability of horizon-optimal fixed quotes. For a known deterministic path or exogenous law, require that over long horizon ranges there is a single quote, or slowly changing quote schedule, that is near-optimal for every prefix in the range. Try a doubling or follow-the-known-value algorithm and test it on sparse spikes, predictable power-of-two spikes, and the same-path 0^N,1/2,1 obstruction.","title":"Horizon-Stability Sufficiency","why_promising":"It explains why horizon-aware oracle policies collapse while horizon-oblivious policies can fail: the issue is rapid movement of the optimal comparator across adjacent horizons."},{"id":"idea_04","risks":"This changes the problem statement. Beta still may be too crude because sparse or low-payoff long delays can survive under pathwise regret too.","suggested_lenses":["model variant","lower-bound construction","quantifier audit"],"summary":"Investigate the alternative regret notion E[sup_a path payoff] minus learner reward. Under this pathwise comparator, the horizon/process-aware pseudo-regret collapse no longer applies, and stochastic mixtures of two continuations may become genuinely hard. Build hidden-state threshold examples and ask whether payoff-relevant long-tail information becomes necessary under this stronger comparator.","title":"Pathwise-Regret Variant","why_promising":"It may recover a meaningful necessity question close to the original intuition, while cleanly explaining why the current pseudo-regret formulation is too weak."},{"id":"idea_05","risks":"The construction must prevent rare branches from creating large expected comparator value at selected horizons. If branch identity reveals the optimal quote, it may reduce to the existing coded-delay example rather than a new obstruction.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample search","horizon calculation","essential-sup pathology"],"summary":"Complete a rare-branch process where beta or gamma stays large because every large delay occurs on some positive-probability history, but the total expected regret contribution is negligible. Tune branch probabilities p_m, delay lengths L_m, and payoffs so that essential-sup conditional tails fail for all h while a simple learner has o(T) expected pseudo-regret for all horizons.","title":"Rare-History Essential-Sup Counterexample","why_promising":"It would show that uniform conditional essential sup is the wrong strength even when delays have positive payoff scale, pushing the theory toward averaged, quantile, or value-of-information tails."},{"id":"idea_06","risks":"Most delayed-feedback literature is bandit or OCO rather than public-price full-information market making. Mapping skipped observations to quote fills must handle horizon censoring and counterfactual rewards exactly.","suggested_lenses":["literature mapping","algorithmic reduction","adaptive truncation"],"summary":"Translate arbitrary-delay skipping and capacity-constrained delayed-learning algorithms to the finite-grid market-making reduction. Aim for a regret bound in terms of skipped payoff mass, outstanding unresolved quote count, or total value-weighted delay, rather than a fixed H and T beta(H). Use public prices to compute truncated counterfactual grid rewards once they resolve, and define skip penalties at comparator level.","title":"Adaptive Skipping With Value-Weighted Feedback","why_promising":"The literature increasingly measures missing information and skipped feedback rather than elapsed delay; this aligns with the counterexamples showing raw tails are too pessimistic."},{"id":"idea_07","risks":"Improved discretization may not affect the necessity question, and adversarial step functions may still force fine grids in worst case.","suggested_lenses":["threshold geometry","nonuniform grids","algorithmic improvement"],"summary":"Exploit the structure r_t(a) = a times an indicator that a is below a future maximum. The fixed-action value is a decreasing-threshold step objective of the form a N_T(a). Explore nonuniform or adaptive grids, critical-price compression, and one-sided Lipschitz/posting-price techniques to replace the crude T/K discretization term by a data-dependent bound.","title":"Continuous Threshold Geometry","why_promising":"The current sufficiency theorem treats quotes as generic experts and ignores strong monotone threshold geometry that may yield sharper algorithms and cleaner comparator approximations."}],"solution_status":"unsolved"}
./working_notes/failed_attempts.md:57:Failed under the current pseudo-regret. The equal-revenue or other dual distribution over terminal thresholds is a minimax certificate showing that some deterministic continuation is bad for a common prefix action law. If that threshold is instead sampled as a stochastic process and regret remains \(\sup_a\mathbb E[\text{fixed-action reward}]-\mathbb E[\text{learner reward}]\), a learner can target the best fixed quote in expectation. This repeats the stochastic-mixture trap from FA-009.
./working_notes/failed_attempts.md:69:Zero atoms should be conditioned away first because they only scale the gap; counting a zero atom as one of the \(m\) support points changes the convention. Likewise, under strict crossing, support prices themselves need not attain the revenue; the proof uses left limits \(a\uparrow c_i\). The dual equal-revenue law remains a deterministic-continuation certificate, not a hard stochastic instance under pseudo-regret.
