./logs/iter_001_synthesis.stderr.log:843:iterations/iter_001/promising_ideas.json:1:{"bibliography_candidates":[{"authors":"Pooria Joulani, Andras Gyorgy, Csaba Szepesvari","relevance":"Primary black-box delayed-feedback reduction for online learning; supports the solver's finite-grid truncated expert upper bound.","title":"Online Learning under Delayed Feedback","url":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v28/joulani13.html","venue_year":"ICML / PMLR 2013"},{"authors":"Kent Quanrud, Daniel Khashabi","relevance":"Gives full-information adversarial-delay regret in terms of cumulative delay, relevant to replacing a fixed truncation horizon by delay-adaptive bounds.","title":"Online Learning with Adversarial Delays","url":"https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper_files/paper/2015/hash/72da7fd6d1302c0a159f6436d01e9eb0-Abstract.html","venue_year":"NeurIPS 2015"},{"authors":"Tobias Sommer Thune, Nicolo Cesa-Bianchi, Yevgeny Seldin","relevance":"Introduces unrestricted-delay bandit techniques and skipping of excessively delayed feedback, a likely template for sharper market-making conditions.","title":"Nonstochastic Multiarmed Bandits with Unrestricted Delays","url":"https://papers.nips.cc/paper/8881-nonstochastic-multiarmed-bandits-with-unrestricted-delays","venue_year":"NeurIPS 2019"},{"authors":"Julian Zimmert, Yevgeny Seldin","relevance":"Optimal arbitrary-delay adversarial bandit bounds with refined skip-set guarantees; useful terminology for excessive-delay robustness.","title":"An Optimal Algorithm for Adversarial Bandits with Arbitrary Delays","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.06054","venue_year":"AISTATS 2020"},{"authors":"Andras Gyorgy, Pooria Joulani","relevance":"Delay- and data-adaptive adversarial bandit bounds, including skipping; may inspire bounds depending on delayed payoff mass rather than unweighted delay probability.","title":"Adapting to Delays and Data in Adversarial Multi-Armed Bandits","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2010.06022","venue_year":"ICML 2021"},{"authors":"Tal Lancewicki, Shahar Segal, Tomer Koren, Yishay Mansour","relevance":"Uses delay quantiles and permits infinite delays in stochastic bandits; relevant to replacing uniform tail assumptions by quantile/truncation penalties.","title":"Stochastic Multi-Armed Bandits with Unrestricted Delay Distributions","url":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v139/lancewicki21a.html","venue_year":"ICML / PMLR 2021"},{"authors":"Genevieve E. Flaspohler, Francesco Orabona, Judah Cohen, Soukayna Mouatadid, Miruna Oprescu, Paulo Orenstein, Lester Mackey","relevance":"Develops optimistic delayed online-learning algorithms and reductions, potentially useful for tuning-free full-information expert implementations.","title":"Online Learning with Optimism and Delay","url":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v139/flaspohler21a.html","venue_year":"ICML / PMLR 2021"},{"authors":"Paul Duetting, Guru Guruganesh, Jon Schneider, Joshua Ruizhi Wang","relevance":"One-sided Lipschitz maximization is structurally close to threshold/posting-price reward curves and may help improve discretization or exploit monotonicity.","title":"Optimal No-Regret Learning for One-Sided Lipschitz Functions","url":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v202/duetting23b.html","venue_year":"ICML / PMLR 2023"},{"authors":"Lukas Zierahn, Dirk van der Hoeven, Tal Lancewicki, Aviv Rosenberg, Nicolo Cesa-Bianchi","relevance":"Recent unified delayed-feedback analysis for nonstochastic bandit settings; useful for modern delay decomposition and total-delay terminology.","title":"A Unified Analysis of Nonstochastic Delayed Feedback for Combinatorial Semi-Bandits, Linear Bandits, and MDPs","url":"https://www.jmlr.org/papers/v26/24-0496.html","venue_year":"JMLR 2025"}],"ideas":[{"id":"idea_01","risks":"If the intended theorem is minimax over a class of processes rather than literal per-process learnability, the sparse-spike counterexample may only expose a quantifier mismatch.","suggested_lenses":["proof audit","quantifier analysis","counterexample generalization"],"summary":"Verify the solver's sparse vanishing-spike refutation line by line: beta(h)=1 via action a=0 at spike times, the best fixed comparator has expected payoff at most 1 for every horizon, and the continuum maximum exists. Then try to strengthen it to a family with beta=1 and regret bounded by any prescribed sublinear envelope.","title":"Audit the Refutation","why_promising":"This is the shortest path to a rigorous conclusion about the stated iff: the unweighted tail condition appears to count long delays at actions with negligible payoff."},{"id":"idea_02","risks":"The essential supremum over histories may still be too strong; rare histories or isolated times can violate gamma without causing linear expected regret.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","condition refinement","counterexample search"],"summary":"Replace beta by the payoff-weighted tail gamma(h)=sup_t sup_a esssup a Pr(h<tau_t(a)<infty | history). Prove the same upper bound with T gamma(H) in place of T beta(H), and show equivalence to uniform tightness over positive-scale actions a>=epsilon for every epsilon>0. Then test whether gamma(h)->0 is necessary or only sufficient.","title":"Payoff-Weighted Tail","why_promising":"It directly repairs the solver's obstruction: long delays at a=0 or tiny quote levels should not matter for regret."},{"id":"idea_03","risks":"Necessity may fail because an algorithm can exploit predictable price structure rather than waiting for delayed reward feedback.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","necessity test","rare-history examples"],"summary":"Study the ex-ante horizon-level truncation error Delta_T(H)=sup_a sum_{t<=T} E[a 1{H<tau_t(a)<=T-t}]. Establish sufficiency when some H_T has H_T log T=o(T) and Delta_T(H_T)=o(T), using the same delayed-grid reduction. Investigate whether this is closer to a true characterization than uniform gamma.","title":"Horizon Tail Error","why_promising":"This is the weakest condition visible from the truncation proof and avoids beta's worst-case sup over irrelevant times and histories."},{"id":"idea_04","risks":"Failure of beta alone is too weak for lower bounds; any theorem will likely need positive payoff scale, positive density in time, and unpredictability assumptions.","suggested_lenses":["lower bound","Yao construction","indistinguishability"],"summary":"If the intended statement is uniform over a class of processes, formulate a lower-bo so that \((M_i+m_i)/N_i\to0\). For any process-aware but horizon-oblivious policy, CL-026 and the equal-revenue value from CL-023 give some \(j_i\) with
